summary: Issue 1891 - registrar fails to parse EK certificates wrapped in ASN.1 OCTET STRING
description: |
    Some hardware TPMs store the EK certificate in NV RAM wrapped in an
    ASN.1 OCTET STRING (tag 0x04) instead of raw DER (tag 0x30 SEQUENCE),
    per a convention mentioned in the TCG EK Credential Profile.
    Obtains a real EK certificate and AK from the TPM, wraps the EK
    certificate's DER bytes in an OCTET STRING, and registers a fake agent
    directly against the registrar's HTTP API with the wrapped certificate,
    bypassing the agent entirely.
    A control agent is registered with the same, non-wrapped EK certificate
    to confirm the test setup itself is sound.
    Completes registration for both agents (decrypting the registrar's
    challenge with the shared EK/AK and activating, like a real agent would)
    and checks that both reach the Registered state, confirming that the
    registrar genuinely accepts the OCTET-STRING-wrapped EK certificate the
    same way it accepts the non-wrapped one, not just that the initial POST
    returns 200.
contact: Marek Safarik <msafarik@redhat.com>
component:
  - keylime
test: ./test.sh
framework: beakerlib
require:
  - gawk
  - jq
  - openssl
  - python3
  - python3-pyasn1
  - tpm2-tools
recommend:
  - keylime
duration: 5m
enabled: true
id: 404a06ad-01e5-4685-a401-51ad939c8b20

adjust:
  - when: swtpm is not defined
    enabled: false
    because: This test needs a TPM (emulated or hardware) to obtain a real EK certificate
